{"step_index":170,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T07:56:07Z","thinking":"The previous `git clone` succeeded because the repository is public, thus authentication wasn't required.  Pushing operations, however, necessitate credentials over HTTPS, as indicated by the Git prompts for username and password. The user's statement about changing the GitHub account credentials directly relates to the need for updated authentication for Git operations.\n\n\nGit is prompting for credentials because none are configured for `github.com`. GitHub now mandates either a Personal Access Token (PAT) or an SSH key for authentication when pushing via HTTPS, as regular passwords are no longer accepted. Two options exist to resolve this: using a PAT, or setting up an SSH key.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"/home/koby/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub","toolAction":"Viewing public key","toolSummary":"View ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub"}}]}
