{"step_index":196,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-17T08:07:40Z","content":"To perform a `git push` manually from your terminal, follow these steps:\n\n---\n\n### Standard Workflow\n\n#### 1. Navigate to the repository\n```bash\ncd /home/koby/LINUX\n```\n\n#### 2. Check your changes & branch\n```bash\ngit status\n```\n\n#### 3. Stage & commit your modifications (when you make changes)\n```bash\n# Stage specific files or all modified files\ngit add .\n\n# Commit with a descriptive message\ngit commit -m \"feat(playbooks): update server configuration\"\n```\n\n#### 4. Push to GitHub\nBecause your upstream tracking is already set to `origin/Baba-Koby`, you can simply run:\n```bash\ngit push\n```\nOr specify the remote and branch explicitly:\n```bash\ngit push origin Baba-Koby\n```\n\n---\n\n### How Authentication Works Behind the Scenes\n\nYou don't need to type your token or password each time because:\n1. [`~/.gitconfig`](file:///home/koby/.gitconfig) has `credential.helper = store`.\n2. Your token is safely cached in [`~/.git-credentials`](file:///home/koby/.git-credentials).\n3. Whenever you run `git push`, Git reads the token for `github.com` and authenticates automatically.\n\n---\n\n### Helpful Tips\n\n* **Pushing a brand new branch**:\n  If you ever create a new branch locally (e.g. `feat/new-playbook`):\n  ```bash\n  git checkout -b feat/new-playbook\n  git push -u origin feat/new-playbook\n  ```\n  *(The `-u` / `--set-upstream` flag links it to GitHub so future pushes only require typing `git push`)*.\n\n* **Checking sync status before pushing**:\n  ```bash\n  git fetch origin\n  git status\n  ```","thinking":"To manually push changes, the user first needs to navigate to the correct directory using a command. Next, checking the repository's status and current branch is essential before proceeding. If there are uncommitted changes, they'll need to be staged and committed.\n\n\n"}
